# OpenKita AI – core rules

OpenKita AI is an open-source, self-hosted, local-first AI assistant for German Kitas.
It is **not** a Kita management system.

## Product boundaries
- No child profiles. No automated assessment of, or decision about, children or staff.
- AI produces **drafts**. Any consequential action (send, publish, create/modify/delete outside the draft) needs explicit human approval.
- Never let generated text claim an action was performed. Confirmations come from code results.
- Knowledge answers cite sources and say when the information is not in the documents. Never invent policies.

## Privacy (fail closed)
- v0.1–v0.2: AI runs only on the local provider. External AI, the MCP server and the MCP client are refused at startup.
- From v0.3, all data leaving the host (external AI, MCP) goes through the Privacy Gateway (`app/security`, planned; ADR 0003). No code path bypasses it.
- Only LOW-risk, pseudonymised data may leave the host. Special-category data and MEDIUM/HIGH/UNKNOWN never do.
- If a privacy, permission, validation or audit step errors or times out, refuse the action.
- Never put real personal data (children, parents, staff) in code, tests, fixtures, examples, logs or commits. Use clearly fictional data.
- Audit logs hold metadata only, never prompt or document content.

## Architecture
- Open source and self-hostable. No mandatory proprietary or cloud dependency. Check licences before adding deps.
- Local-first AI via Ollama behind the provider interface (`app/ai/base.py`). Never import a vendor SDK in services.
- All config via environment variables. Every new variable goes into `.env.example` with a safe default and no secrets.
- German is the default UI and output language. All UI strings go through i18n catalogs.
- No placeholder buttons or controls that do nothing. Unbuilt features show an honest "coming in a later version" state.

## Way of working
- Build incrementally. Keep the existing layering (`api → services → ai/models/documents/security`).
  Planned packages `skills`, `tools` (central executor) and `mcp` sit between `services` and `ai/security` (see `docs/prd-addendum-skills-mcp.md`).
- Roadmap: `docs/PRD.md` §7 is canonical. Don't build features ahead of their version.
- Add tests for every implemented behaviour, including failure paths.
- Record architectural decisions as ADRs in `docs/decisions/NNNN-title.md`.
- Update `docs/architecture.md` when structure changes. Mark unbuilt parts as **planned**.
